Phenomenon Money - What's Behind It

The mysterious phenomenon of money


Why almost nobody sees through the design flaw of the monetary system


You will certainly have heard one of the recurring news reports from time to time: the gap between rich and poor has widened again. Regardless of whether the economy is stable or not, unemployment is high or low: this phenomenon does not change at all and it seems like a kind of law of nature - which it is not.


According to the natural law of interaction, no effect can occur without a clear cause. Of course, this is also the case with all the distortions in the monetary system and the resulting ever-increasing social tensions, which arise, among other things, because many hard-working but low-earning people see themselves deprived of a large part of the wages they are entitled to. There is therefore a specific error hidden in the monetary system, but one that has never been seriously looked for for thousands of years.


Isn't it strange that almost all people know next to nothing about the nature of money, which literally rules the world? Yet it is a psychological law that when we are completely uninformed, we can be controlled by others with the greatest of ease ! And that is actually the case: the huge distortion in the monetary system that arises when an essentially simple topic is made incomprehensible with increasingly complicated financial models enables a small minority to build up gigantic amounts of wealth at the expense of the general public and thus control it! In doing so, they cleverly let the exploited believe that they are profiting themselves, although of course the opposite is the case!


This design flaw in the monetary system was discovered at the beginning of the 20th century by the German-Argentinian Silvio Gesell, but his findings have remained a niche issue to this day and are ignored despite their enormous importance. Instead, the majority of people prefer to occupy themselves with trivialities and gossip, which cannot contribute in the slightest to a better world.


The following articles take a holistic look at the invention of money, which in its current form causes far greater damage than almost anyone realizes. They may be from 2009, but the fundamental systemic flaw in money has not changed for thousands of years . So it should come as no surprise that nothing has improved since 2009 - on the contrary: the situation has worsened even if we have not often been aware of this in recent years due to a strong economy. That is why the following articles are still highly topical, despite a few small changes in the financial system and slightly changed data. They touch on fundamental questions about how we interact in the economic and trade cycle and show unexpected solutions.


